    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1223">

                <pb facs="0997" n="987" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg987"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 Ward, Angus I.: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Peiping</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Clubb</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-10-28">October 28,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received October 27—8:27 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1839. ReContel 1837, October 27. Following received late yesterday evening as
                    Mukden’s 89, 24th: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Consul General Ward, Rehberg, Tatsumi and two European employees removed
                            by police for 2 or 3 days connection their alleged as <pb facs="0998"
                                n="988" xml:id="pg988"/> sault Chinese employee. Sent Washington,
                            repeated Peiping 89. Stokes<anchor n="16" xml:id="d1223-f1"/> for
                            Ward.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Repeated hereby despite original routing provide against possible transmittal
                    delay.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 1839, Shanghai 1106.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Clubb</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1223-f1" n="16" xml:id="d1223-f1-content">
                <p>William N. Stokes, Vice Consul at Mukden.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
